   "subject": "dplyr mutate use cases",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Why would I choose to use mutate() instead of something like:<div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">df$newvar </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#008\">with</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> df</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> var1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> var2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><font color=\"#666600\"></font></div></code></div><br>Or in general, what are use cases for mutate that maybe aren&#39;t readily apparent.  </div><div><br></div><div>robbie</div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ziHR0Os57KgJ",
   "from": "Andrew Lim",
   "author": "3cfd637695216907",
   "date": "2014-12-10T14:13:00Z",
   "subject": "Re: dplyr mutate use cases",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div>Works with group_by, also easier syntactically to stack with the %&gt;% operation. </div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> df </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">frame</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> df </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%&gt;%</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dplyr</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">group_by</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%&gt;%</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dplyr</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mutate</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> min</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Source</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">local</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data frame </span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Groups</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x y<br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\">     </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\">     </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\">     </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\">     </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span></div></code></div><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br>On Tuesday, December 9, 2014 4:37:11 PM UTC-5, Robbie Edwards wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Why would I choose to use mutate() instead of something like:<div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">df$newvar </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#008\">with</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> df</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> var1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> var2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><font color=\"#666600\"></font></div></code></div><br>Or in general, what are use cases for mutate that maybe aren&#39;t readily apparent.  </div><div><br></div><div>robbie</div><div><br></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
