  {
   "id": "pLGuaFgr36cJ",
   "from": "Bob",
   "author": "3afa2b67ef806635",
   "date": "2014-12-30T13:28:09Z",
   "subject": "Re: dplyr mutate use cases",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Using with(), you have to repeat the with() call once and the name of the data frame twice for every new variable you create:<div><br></div><div>df$newvar1 &lt;- with(df, var1 + var2)</div><div>df$newvar2 &lt;- with(df, var3 + var4)</div><div>df$newvar3 &lt;- with(df, var5 + var6)</div><div><br></div><div>When using mutate(), you only have to name the data frame twice regardless of how many variables you create. I think within() is closer to mutate() than with() is.</div><div><br></div><div>df &lt;- mutate(df,</div><div>  newvar1 = var1 + var2,</div><div>  newvar2 = var3 + var4,</div><div>  newvar3 = var5 + var6)</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>On Tuesday, December 9, 2014 4:37:11 PM UTC-5, Robbie Edwards wrote:</div></div>"
    ],
